From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 20:09:03 +0900
Subject: Give a method body its repeated parameters through a Gatherable
 instance
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A repeated parameter `xs: T*` is a `Seq[T]` in the body of its method. Code
whose collections are not the standard library's — Soundness hides those
behind opaque aliases — must convert every such parameter by hand before it
can use it.

Under -Zgatherable-varargs, desugaring rewrites

    def f(xs: T*) = body

to

    def f(xs: T*) =
      val xs$1 = xs
      { val xs = scala.Gatherable.gather(xs$1); body }

so the body sees `xs` as the `Out` of the `scala.Gatherable[T]` instance in
scope at the definition, converted once on entry. The implicit search is the
typer's own, at the parameter, and the method does not compile if no instance
is in scope. The signature is unchanged, so callers are unaffected whether or
not they are compiled with the flag.

Constructors, compiler-generated methods, macros, `inline` parameters and
by-name repeated parameters are left as upstream has them.

`Gatherable` ships in the supplementary proscala-library jar. The conversion
goes through the method `Gatherable.gather` rather than the instance's own
member because, under capture checking, a member of a trait compiled without
it cannot be applied at an element type that captures a capability, whereas a
polymorphic method can.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: gatherable
